# Configuration — API.md §Configuration, CONFIGURATION.md
#
# POST is a PARTIAL update, deep-merged onto the effective config, validated,
# written back to /config/fastadhunter.toml, then applied:
#
#   runtime-mutable key -> applied live via atomic swap  -> "applied": true
#   boot-only key       -> persisted, needs a restart    -> "restart_required": true
#
# CAUTION: the file is written from the EFFECTIVE config, so any value that
# came from an environment variable gets baked in permanently. Start the
# container with FAH__DNS__LISTEN__PORT=5353, POST anything here, and the file
# now says 5353 even after you remove the variable.

@base = {{host}}/api/v1
@auth = Authorization: Bearer {{apiKey}}

### Effective config, all sources merged (defaults < file < env < API).
### Secrets are absent by construction — the API key and TLS private key live
### in their own /config files and never enter this tree.
GET {{base}}/config
{{auth}}

### Runtime key — expect { "applied": true, "restart_required": false }.
### Only four keys are runtime (CONFIGURATION.md §Mutability classes): the two
### [history] ones, api.metrics_public, and this. Everything else is read once
### at startup and is therefore boot.
POST {{base}}/config
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "rules": {
    "refresh_hours_default": 24
  }
}

### Boot-only key — expect { "applied": false, "restart_required": true }.
### The value is persisted but the running process keeps the old one. The whole
### of [dns.cache] is boot: DnsCache::new reads it once, at startup, so resizing
### the cache needs a restart. To release cache memory *without* one, use
### POST /cache/clean (cache.http).
POST {{base}}/config
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "dns": {
    "cache": {
      "max_bytes": 33554432
    }
  }
}

### Boot-only key — the listener, same contract.
POST {{base}}/config
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "api": {
    "port": 8443
  }
}

### Mixed patch — expect both flags true. Needs one key from each class, so the
### runtime half has to be a genuinely live key: api.port is boot, and
### dns.cache would NOT work here because it is boot too.
POST {{base}}/config
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "api": { "port": 8443 },
  "history": { "retention_days": 60 }
}

### Nested keys merge; siblings are untouched. Only min_ttl_seconds changes
### here — max_ttl_seconds, serve_stale and the rest survive.
POST {{base}}/config
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "dns": {
    "cache": {
      "min_ttl_seconds": 600
    }
  }
}

### Blocking mode and TTL.
POST {{base}}/config
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "dns": {
    "blocking": {
      "mode": "null_ip",
      "ttl_seconds": 10
    }
  }
}

### Put /health and /metrics behind the API key.
POST {{base}}/config
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "api": {
    "metrics_public": false
  }
}

### Log level — boot: expect { "restart_required": true }. The tracing filter is
### built once at init, so the new level applies at the next start.
POST {{base}}/config
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "log": {
    "level": "debug"
  }
}

### Query log retention — boot: expect { "restart_required": true }. Note these
### caps govern the /data segments, which NO endpoint reads yet; GET /queries
### serves the in-RAM ring only (CONFIGURATION.md §What reads the query log).
POST {{base}}/config
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "query_log": {
    "enabled": true,
    "retention_days": 7,
    "retention_max_mb": 1024
  }
}

### Long-term history retention (the persisted /data/history series behind
### history.http). `enabled` and `retention_days` (30 | 60 | 90) are
### runtime-mutable — expect { "applied": true, "restart_required": false } —
### and a tightened window takes effect on the NEXT prune, deleting day-files
### already past it. Disabling stops both the rollup writers and the perf
### sampler (SECURITY.md: it narrows the retained-data window).
POST {{base}}/config
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "history": {
    "enabled": true,
    "retention_days": 30
  }
}

### The sampling cadence — boot: expect { "restart_required": true }. The running
### sampler builds its ticker once at startup, so the new cadence takes effect at
### the next boot; the persisted value is what that boot uses. (This key used to
### answer `applied: true` while behaving exactly like a boot key — the
### classification now matches the behaviour.)
POST {{base}}/config
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "history": {
    "sample_interval_seconds": 60
  }
}

### Upstream servers. ARRAYS REPLACE WHOLESALE — they do not merge, so send
### the complete set; a partial array silently drops the entries you left out.
### (rules.lists is NOT settable here at all — expect 422. Rule lists are owned
### by the /lists endpoints, which apply live and persist for you: lists.http.)
POST {{base}}/config
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "dns": {
    "upstreams": {
      "strategy": "fallback",
      "timeout_ms": 800,
      "servers": [
        { "address": "1.1.1.1", "protocol": "udp" },
        { "address": "9.9.9.9", "protocol": "udp" }
      ]
    }
  }
}

### Encrypted upstreams (DoT). Opt-in — without them the ISP sees every query
### in plaintext (SECURITY.md §Upstream privacy).
POST {{base}}/config
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "dns": {
    "upstreams": {
      "servers": [
        { "address": "1.1.1.1", "protocol": "dot", "hostname": "cloudflare-dns.com" }
      ]
    }
  }
}

### Unknown key — expect 422. `deny_unknown_fields` turns a typo into an error
### rather than a silently ignored setting, and NOTHING is persisted.
POST {{base}}/config
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "dns": {
    "cache": {
      "max_entrees": 1
    }
  }
}

### Semantically invalid — expect 422 naming the constraint. A rejected patch
### leaves both the file and the running config untouched.
POST {{base}}/config
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "dns": {
    "listen": {
      "port": 0
    }
  }
}

### No-op patch — same value as current. Expect both flags false.
POST {{base}}/config
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "dns": {
    "cache": {
      "max_entries": 50000
    }
  }
}
